$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Oxidation of 3,4-dimethoxybenzyl alcohol (7a) and dimer model compound
(1) on 10 g and 5 g scale: To a 1 L round-bottom, three-necked flask
equipped with a stir bar was added veratryl alcohol (59.4 mmol, 10 g) or
dimeric model compound 1 (15 mmol, 5.1 g), 5 mol % of 4-acetamido-TEMPO,
200 mL acetonitrile and 6 mL water. The flask was equipped with a
condenser and sealed with rubber septum. Oxygen gas was bubbled through
the solution for ca. 10 minutes. Then, 10 mol% of nitric acid (67%) and
10 mol% of hydrochloric acid (37%) were injected through the septum. In
addition, four balloons of oxygen attached to a syringe needle were
attached to the top of septa. The reaction mixture was stirred at 45 °C
and monitored by TLC. These reactions took 3 h and 9 h for 7a and 1,
respectively. The solvent was evaporated and the residue was washed with
aqueous solution of NaHCO3 (10 %) and was extracted with ethyl acetate.
The organic layers were combined, dried over MgSO4, filtered and
concentrated under vacuum. The residue was subjected to column
chromatography to obtain the corresponding carbonyl compound in 94% and
96% isolated yield of 8a and 3, respectively. 3,4-Dimethoxy-benzaldehyde
(8a): Chromatography solvent: hexanes:ethyl acetate (8:1). Spectral data
are consistent with those reported in the literature.12 Yield 94%.
Spectral data are consistent with those reported in the literature.12 1H
NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 9.86 (s, 1H), 7.47 (dd, J = 8.2, 2.0 Hz, 1H), 7.41
(d, J = 1.8 Hz, 1H), 6.97 (d, J = 8.2 Hz, 1H), 3.97 (s, 3H), 3.95 (s,
3H). HRMS (EI) calculated for C9H10O3 [M]+ 166.0630, found 166.0626.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative reaction conditions shown, selective for aldehyde
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Chemoselective Metal-Free Aerobic Alcohol Oxidation in Lignin
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Rahimi, Alireza; Azarpira, Ali; Kim, Hoon; Ralph, John; Stahl, Shannon S.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2013), 135(17), 6415-6418

